  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist, and the rule is enabled and reports status `ok`.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-f78ab690 Web logs overview\"** (ID `eff-f78ab690-web-logs-overview-95a4197d4e62`)\n- Total requests (metric, count)\n- Average response size in bytes (metric, average of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time (line, date histogram on `@timestamp`)\n- Requests by response code (pie, terms on `response.keyword`, top 10)\n- Time range: last 1 year. I picked this because I couldn't confirm the sample data's timestamps.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-f78ab690 5xx spike\"** (ID `f7de9b6b-65c4-4107-9bec-fe38e26897ba`)\n- Type `.es-query`, runs every 1m, enabled.\n- Counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5*` in a 5-minute window, and fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- I also tagged it `EFF-f78ab690`.\n\n**Data exploration was limited.** `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` is not set in my shell, so I couldn't run raw queries to pull live value counts. I worked from the data view's field list instead: `response` and `bytes` are the key fields, and `response` is a string, so the 5xx match uses a wildcard on `response.keyword`. I did not confirm that every response value is a three-digit code.\n\n**Things to check:**\n- The sample data is probably older than the 5-minute window, so the rule may not fire unless new 5xx traffic arrives.\n- I did not verify the dashboard's panels by rendering them in the browser.",
